1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,341
18%. One number in an earnings story.

2
00:00:04,691 --> 00:00:07,681
If it's wrong, it becomes a public correction.

3
00:00:08,581 --> 00:00:13,173
And AI-written drafts make numbers cheap to produce, and costly to check.

4
00:00:13,273 --> 00:00:19,848
Financial journalists and analysts trace each figure back to SEC filings by hand, one filing at a time.

5
00:00:20,448 --> 00:00:23,738
NoCap gives every number a receipt from the filing.

6
00:00:23,738 --> 00:00:26,614
Models propose; code decides.

7
00:00:27,414 --> 00:00:29,324
This is the real interface.

8
00:00:29,324 --> 00:00:33,564
An AI-drafted NVIDIA story with two planted errors. Check.

9
00:00:33,564 --> 00:00:35,954
Seven verified. Two wrong.

10
00:00:35,954 --> 00:00:39,168
Two unverifiable, because NoCap never guesses.

11
00:00:39,168 --> 00:00:42,678
The draft says revenue rose 18% from a year ago.

12
00:00:42,678 --> 00:00:47,807
The receipt does the math from the filings: 105.85%.

13
00:00:47,807 --> 00:00:49,637
The 18% was quarter over quarter.

14
00:00:49,637 --> 00:00:55,675
Each receipt names the filing, its accession number, and when EDGAR accepted it.

15
00:00:55,675 --> 00:01:00,205
GAAP earnings per diluted share: the draft says $2.22.

16
00:01:00,205 --> 00:01:02,827
The filing says $2.46.

17
00:01:02,827 --> 00:01:09,184
Fix with receipts. Muse Wonder proposes the corrections, and code lets only the digits change.

18
00:01:09,184 --> 00:01:11,474
Then the whole draft is checked again:

19
00:01:11,474 --> 00:01:13,657
nine verified, zero wrong.

20
00:01:13,657 --> 00:01:18,721
Time travel. A June-quarter roundup, checked as of July 29.

21
00:01:18,721 --> 00:01:22,991
A figure whose filing wasn't public yet is look-ahead, not wrong:

22
00:01:22,991 --> 00:01:26,470
six verified, one wrong, two look-ahead.

23
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:29,060
A real Bank of America earnings slide.

24
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:34,426
SAM 3.1 finds the chart regions, and Muse Spark reads 32 printed labels.

25
00:01:34,426 --> 00:01:41,762
Ask a whole filing. Muse Spark reads Meta's entire 10-K, about 101,000 tokens, in one request.

26
00:01:41,762 --> 00:01:45,044
Code keeps only the quotes it finds word for word.

27
00:01:45,044 --> 00:01:51,214
Private mode: the same check on open weights, Muse Glimmer, on the operator's own Modal GPU.

28
00:01:51,214 --> 00:01:53,762
The draft never leaves that deployment.

29
00:01:54,012 --> 00:01:57,222
Under the hood, four Meta models.

30
00:01:57,222 --> 00:02:01,087
Muse Spark 1.3 extracts typed claims and plans the lookups.

31
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:05,317
Deterministic code decides, against SEC EDGAR:

32
00:02:05,317 --> 00:02:10,309
value, period, scale, GAAP or non-GAAP, company, and a point-in-time gate.

33
00:02:10,309 --> 00:02:14,419
Muse Wonder 1.0 proposes corrections that code re-checks.

34
00:02:14,419 --> 00:02:19,349
SAM 3.1 and Muse Spark read slides. Muse Glimmer runs Private.

35
00:02:20,049 --> 00:02:25,329
It covers all 500 S&P 500 companies, the latest eight fiscal quarters.

36
00:02:25,329 --> 00:02:33,093
In a one-shot test on 1,000 fresh items from 400 companies, NoCap caught 306 of 360 planted errors.

37
00:02:33,093 --> 00:02:39,157
It marked none of them verified, and flagged 17 of 1,298 true figures as wrong.

38
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:42,257
Try it live through November 17.

39
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:48,146
NoCap, built by Robert Chang during the Meta Global AI Developer Hackathon.
